# Comment style

Keep type/member summary comments short and push mechanics to the point of use.

- A summary / XML `<summary>` doc block is **5 lines max**. State what the thing
  is and why it exists — not how it works step by step.
- Do not narrate the implementation in the header. If a mechanism needs
  explaining, use **short inline comments inside the method** at the line that
  does the interesting work.
- One idea per inline comment. Prefer a single line over a paragraph.

## Example

```csharp
// BAD - 16-line header narrating the whole mechanism
/// <summary>
/// Base class for game-wide services that must survive every scene load and of
/// which there is exactly one for the whole session (see GameManager's list).
/// First-wins: the first instance to Awake claims Instance, reparents to the
/// scene root, and is marked DontDestroyOnLoad. Any later duplicate destroys its
/// own GameObject. The static is cleared only by the instance that owns it, so
/// repeated additive load/unload cannot leave a stale reference behind.
/// Scene-bound managers must NOT use this ... (etc)
/// </summary>
public abstract class PersistentSingleton<T> : MonoBehaviour where T : PersistentSingleton<T>

// GOOD - short header, mechanics inline where they happen
/// <summary>
/// Game-wide service that survives scene loads; exactly one per session.
/// Not for scene-bound managers (there can be several of those at once).
/// </summary>
public abstract class PersistentSingleton<T> : MonoBehaviour where T : PersistentSingleton<T>
{
    protected virtual void Awake()
    {
        // First-wins: a later duplicate destroys itself.
        if (Instance != null && Instance != this) { Destroy(gameObject); return; }
        Instance = (T)this;
        DontDestroyOnLoad(gameObject);
    }
}
```
